WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.970
A radioactive parent isotope changes into a daughter product.

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:07.070
After one half-life, half the original parents remain.

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:08.870
After two, one quarter remains.

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:12.650
Each equal interval removes half of what is left, not the

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:16.350
same number of atoms. The green parent population shrinks while purple

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:19.570
daughters accumulate in this ideal closed-system reconstruction.

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:24.730
A mass spectrometer ionizes a sample and separates ions by their

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:26.790
behavior in electric and magnetic fields.

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:30.950
A detector counts selected ions, allowing isotope ratios to be measured.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:34.650
The ratio becomes an age only with a suitable decay system

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:40.170
and evaluated assumptions. Report analytical uncertainty along with the age.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:45.800
Potassium-argon and argon-argon systems can date volcanic rocks and cooling.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:49.420
Rubidium-strontium helped establish ancient granite ages.

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:52.400
Uranium and thorium decay chains end in lead;

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:56.420
uranium-lead dating of zircon is especially useful for crystallization.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:00.580
Samarium-neodymium supports ancient-rock and magma-source studies.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:05.360
Carbon-fourteen suits much younger carbon-bearing material, not ancient granite.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:10.040
A volcanic unit cools rapidly, so a suitable date commonly records

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:14.380
eruption. A zircon in granite can record crystallization, while biotite can

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:16.660
record later cooling through its retention temperature.

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:19.260
Metamorphic minerals may date metamorphism.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:23.140
Two minerals from one rock can therefore yield different, meaningful ages

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:25.280
without either measurement simply being wrong.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.980
A grain eroded from an old source can be deposited much

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:32.600
later in sediment. Its isotopic age can date the source crystal

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.060
rather than the younger sedimentary bed.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:39.840
Evaluate initial daughter composition, isotope loss or gain, and the event

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.820
that closed the clock. Numerical dating complements relative relationships;

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:45.460
it does not replace them.
